@page "/async"

<PageTitle>Async rules</PageTitle>
<h1>Async rules</h1>
<p>Type "admin" and pause: each keystroke starts the check immediately (the delay simulates a server call — 600 ms by default, adjustable below), shows a pending state, and a new keystroke cancels the in-flight check.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Handle.cs", "MemoizedHandleValidator.cs", "AsyncRules.razor", "AsyncRules.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Username</strong>: checked for uniqueness asynchronously while you type (a delay stands in for the server — 600&nbsp;ms by default, adjustable below). Taken usernames: <code>admin</code>, <code>root</code>, <code>formidable</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Display name</strong>: checked for uniqueness the same way, independently of Username. Taken display names: <code>Administrator</code>, <code>Root User</code>, <code>Formidable</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Username</strong>: also required to submit, and the live channel answers that rule too: clear a username you have already typed and "Username is required" lands with no submit anywhere.</li>
            <li>Editing after a submit also schedules a background refresh (300&nbsp;ms) that reconciles the whole form — but the pending indicator stays on the field you edited, before and after submit alike.</li>
            <li>Editing after a submit still runs each check once. The refresh executes only the rules still owed an answer at the current edit, so a live pass that has already landed leaves it nothing to run; and where the two overlap — a check still in flight when the refresh fires — the memo below joins the call in progress instead of starting a second.</li>
            <li>Both uniqueness checks also memoize their answer for 10&nbsp;seconds: type <code>admin</code>, clear it, then retype it, and the second check comes back from that memo instead of running again — the same memo that lets a Submit pressed shortly after a live pass skip a repeat round trip too.</li>
            <li><strong>Validator override</strong>: <code>FormidableForm</code>'s <code>Validator</code> parameter resolves from DI when left unset, which is what every other page here does; this page supplies <code>MemoizedHandleValidator</code> directly instead, which is why the memoizing shows up here rather than on <code>/field-state</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>LiveDebounce</strong>: batches keystrokes. Tick the checkbox below and a burst of typing starts one check after you pause, instead of one per keystroke. It is a separate clock from the delay above — the delay sets how long a check takes once it starts; LiveDebounce sets when one starts. After a submit, the same edit also arms the refresh; whichever of the two runs first pays for the check, and the other reuses its answer instead of running it again — ticking the box costs nothing extra there either.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Type <code>admin</code> and pause — a spinner and "checking…" appear on that field only, then the taken message lands.</li>
            <li>Type quickly — each keystroke cancels the in-flight check; only the final value gets a verdict.</li>
            <li>Type in Display name — its indicator runs independently; the pending state is scoped to the field being edited.</li>
            <li>Clear Username — "Username is required" appears as soon as that edit's live pass does, with no submit: you engaged the field, and the live channel runs the rules a submit would. Give it a beat if Display name still holds what you typed in the last step, since the same pass waits on its check too. Submit anyway and the same rule blocks.</li>
            <li>Drag the delay to 2000 ms and type — the slider only changes how long the check itself takes, not when it starts or how often; watch the pending state hold for the full two seconds before the verdict lands.</li>
            <li>Submit, then type again — one "checking…" cycle, not two, even though editing after a submit also arms the background refresh (see above).</li>
            <li>Tick <em>Debounce live checks</em>, then type <code>formidable</code> fast — no "checking…" flashes mid-keystroke. Pausing lands only one check here too: the refresh window is the narrower one, so the refresh pays the single round trip, and the debounced live pass that follows finds the check already answered and skips its own — the same saving an ordinary post-submit edit gets without debounce ticked at all.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<p>
    <label>Simulated delay
        <input type="range" min="0" max="2000" step="100" @bind="DelayMs" @bind:event="oninput" />
    </label>
    @HandleValidator.SimulatedDelayMs ms
</p>

<p>
    <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="LiveDebounceEnabled" /> Debounce live checks (batch fast typing into one pass)</label>
</p>

<FormidableForm Model="_handle" Options="_options" Validator="_validator" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Username <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.Username" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
            @if (field.State.IsValidating)
            {
                <em role="status">checking…</em>
            }
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.Username" />
    </FormidableField>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Display name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.DisplayName" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
            @if (field.State.IsValidating)
            {
                <em role="status">checking…</em>
            }
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.DisplayName" />
    </FormidableField>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>

<p><small>The pending flag is scoped to the field being edited during a live pass — type in Username or Display name and only that field's "checking…" indicator shows, not both.</small></p>
